/-!
# A countable directed subsystem with the same colimit

Let `R` be a ring and let `F : J ⥤ ModuleCat R` be a directed system `(M i, f i j)` of `R`-modules
over a nonempty directed preorder `J`, with colimit cocone `c`, so that `M = c.pt` is
`colim_{i ∈ J} M i` with canonical maps `f i = c.ι.app i`. This file proves Stacks 059W: if the
system factors eventually — for every `i` some transition map `f i j` factors through `f i k` for
all `k ≥ i`, which is condition (3) of Stacks 059E — and `M` is generated by countably many
elements, then a countable directed subset `J' ⊆ J` already computes `M`.

The route is the source's. A countable set of indices carrying the generators is enlarged, by
countably many steps, to a countable subset `J'` which is directed and closed under the choice `i ↦
j i` of an index provided by the factoring hypothesis; the two facts about `J'` that this buys are
that the legs `f i`, `i ∈ J'`, are jointly surjective and that an element of `M i`, `i ∈ J'`,
killed by `f i` is killed by a transition map `f i j` with `j ∈ J'`. Those two facts make the
whiskered cocone a colimit cocone, which is
`Stacks.DirectLimit.CountableSubsystem.isColimit_of_iSup_range_of_ker`: for a cocone on a directed
system of modules, joint surjectivity of the legs together with `Ker(f i) = ⋃_{k ≥ i} Ker(f i k)`
is sufficient as well as necessary.

## Main results

* `Stacks.DirectLimit.CountableSubsystem.solution`: Stacks 059W, the statement of
  `ModuleCat.exists_countable_directedOn_isColimit_whisker`.

## Implementation notes

`Mathlib` computes a filtered colimit of modules elementwise only for a diagram indexed by a
category in the universe of the modules, while the statement leaves the index type `J` in its own
universe, so `Module.DirectLimit`, which lives in `Type (max v w)`, is not the point of a cocone in
`ModuleCat.{w} R`. What is proved here instead is that the canonical map out of it,
`Stacks.DirectLimit.CountableSubsystem.directLimitDesc`, is injective —
`Stacks.DirectLimit.CountableSubsystem.injective_directLimitDesc`, the one statement of this file
that has content — and that is what makes an element of `M i` killed by `f i` already killed by a
transition map. The argument: an element of the kernel is detected, if it is nonzero, by a linear
map `Ψ` into an injective object of `ModuleCat.{w} R` receiving the cyclic submodule it generates,
which is `w`-small because `R` is
(`Stacks.DirectLimit.CountableSubsystem.exists_linearMap_apply_ne_zero`); such a `Ψ` curries into a
cocone on `F` in `ModuleCat.{w} R`, so it factors through `c.pt` by the universal property
(`Stacks.DirectLimit.CountableSubsystem.exists_comp_directLimitOf`), and therefore kills the
element after all. This is the only use of `Small.{w} R`, and the reason the argument does not need
`J` to be small: the injective object is a single module, not a colimit. Joint surjectivity of the
legs, on the other hand, needs no smallness: the quotient of `c.pt` by the supremum of their ranges
receives both the zero map and the projection, which the universal property forces to agree.

Together with `Stacks.DirectLimit.CountableSubsystem.exists_legHom_eq` the injectivity says that
`c.pt` *is* the direct limit of `F` computed elementwise, an equivalence `Module.DirectLimit …
≃ₗ[R] c.pt`; only the two directions are used here, so no such equivalence is built.

A leg `c.ι.app i` of a cocone has codomain `((Functor.const J).obj c.pt).obj i`, which is only
definitionally the underlying module of `c.pt`, so a family such as the ranges of the legs is not
even well typed as written; `Stacks.DirectLimit.CountableSubsystem.legHom` is the leg with its
codomain displayed as `↥c.pt`, and it is an `abbrev` because there is nothing to say about it
beyond that.

The countable directed subset is built by
`Stacks.DirectLimit.CountableSubsystem.exists_countable_directedOn_superset`, which is the
combinatorial content of the source's alternating construction: a countable subset of a directed
preorder extends to a countable directed subset closed under a prescribed map. Adjoining the values
of the map and an upper bound for each pair at *every* step, rather than alternating the two
operations, shortens the bookkeeping and gives the same union.

The descent map out of `c.pt`,
`Stacks.DirectLimit.CountableSubsystem.exists_linearMap_comp_legHom`, is built as the graph of the
map to be defined, that is as the supremum `Γ` of the ranges of the maps `(f i, t.ι.app i) : M i →
M × t.pt`: joint surjectivity says the first projection of `Γ` is onto, the description of the
kernels says it is injective, and the second projection of its inverse is the descent map. This
avoids choosing a preimage.

## References

* [StacksProject 059W](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/059W)
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Stacks.DirectLimit.CountableSubsystem

open CategoryTheory Limits



/-! ### An injective object detecting a nonzero element -/

/-- Over a ring which is `w`-small, a nonzero element of a module in an arbitrary universe is
detected by a linear map into an object of `ModuleCat.{w} R`: the cyclic submodule it generates is
`w`-small, hence embeds in an injective object, and the embedding extends to the whole module. -/
private lemma exists_linearMap_apply_ne_zero (R : Type u) [Ring R] [Small.{w} R] {L : Type*}
    [AddCommGroup L] [Module R L] {z : L} (hz : z ≠ 0) :
    ∃ (E : ModuleCat.{w} R) (Ψ : L →ₗ[R] E), Ψ z ≠ 0 := by
  have := Submodule.small_span_singleton (R := R) z
  set X : ModuleCat.{w} R := ModuleCat.of R (Shrink.{w} (R ∙ z : Submodule R L))
  set E : ModuleCat.{w} R := Injective.under X
  have : Module.Injective R (E : Type w) :=
    Module.injective_module_of_injective_object R _
      (inj := (rfl : ModuleCat.of R (E : Type w) = E) ▸ Injective.injective_under X)
  set α : (R ∙ z : Submodule R L) →ₗ[R] (E : Type w) :=
    (Injective.ι X).hom ∘ₗ (Shrink.linearEquiv R (R ∙ z : Submodule R L)).symm.toLinearMap
  have hαinj : Function.Injective α :=
    ((ModuleCat.mono_iff_injective _).1 inferInstance).comp
      (Shrink.linearEquiv R (R ∙ z : Submodule R L)).symm.injective
  obtain ⟨Ψ, hΨ⟩ := Module.Injective.extension_property R (E : Type w) _ _
    (R ∙ z : Submodule R L).subtype Subtype.val_injective α
  refine ⟨E, Ψ, fun h0 => hz ?_⟩
  have hα0 : α ⟨z, Submodule.mem_span_singleton_self z⟩ = 0 := by rw [← hΨ]; simpa using h0
  simpa using hαinj (hα0.trans (map_zero α).symm)

/-! ### A cocone on a directed system of modules -/

section Cocone

variable {R : Type u} [Ring R] {J : Type v} [Preorder J] {F : J ⥤ ModuleCat.{w} R}

/-- The leg at `i` of a cocone on a diagram of `R`-modules, with its codomain the underlying module
of the cocone point rather than the value at `i` of a constant functor. -/
private abbrev legHom (c : Cocone F) (i : J) : (F.obj i : Type w) →ₗ[R] (c.pt : Type w) :=
  (c.ι.app i).hom

/-- Between two objects of the index category of a preorder there is at most one arrow, so a
transition map depends only on its source and its target. -/
private lemma map_eq_map (F : J ⥤ ModuleCat.{w} R) {i j : J} (u v : i ⟶ j) :
    F.map u = F.map v :=
  congrArg F.map (Subsingleton.elim u v)

/-- The legs of a cocone absorb the transition maps. -/
private lemma legHom_map_apply (c : Cocone F) {i j : J} (u : i ⟶ j) (y : F.obj i) :
    legHom c j ((F.map u).hom y) = legHom c i y :=
  congr($(c.w u) y)

/-- A transition map which kills `y` kills every transition map it factors, in particular the
eventual map provided by the Mittag-Leffler hypothesis. -/
private lemma map_eq_zero_of_map_comp_eq {i j k : J} {u : i ⟶ j} {v : i ⟶ k} {h : F.obj k ⟶ F.obj j}
    (hh : F.map u = F.map v ≫ h) {y : F.obj i} (hv : (F.map v).hom y = 0) :
    (F.map u).hom y = 0 := by
  rw [hh]
  simpa using congrArg h.hom hv

/-- `F`, read as an unbundled directed system of `R`-modules. -/
private lemma directedSystem_map (F : J ⥤ ModuleCat.{w} R) :
    DirectedSystem (fun i => (F.obj i : Type w)) fun _ _ h => (F.map (homOfLE h)).hom := by
  refine ⟨fun _ y => by simp, fun a b d hab hbd y => ?_⟩
  exact (congr($((map_eq_map F _ (homOfLE hab ≫ homOfLE hbd)).trans (F.map_comp _ _)) y)).symm

/-- The ranges of a family of maps out of the members of a directed system which absorb the
transition maps form a directed family of submodules. -/
private lemma directed_range_of_forall_comp [IsDirectedOrder J] {P : Type w} [AddCommGroup P]
    [Module R P] (φ : ∀ i : J, (F.obj i : Type w) →ₗ[R] P)
    (hφ : ∀ (i j : J) (u : i ⟶ j) (y : F.obj i), φ j ((F.map u).hom y) = φ i y) :
    Directed (· ≤ ·) fun i : J => LinearMap.range (φ i) := by
  intro i j
  obtain ⟨k, hik, hjk⟩ := exists_ge_ge i j
  refine ⟨k, ?_, ?_⟩ <;> rintro x ⟨y, rfl⟩
  · exact ⟨(F.map (homOfLE hik)).hom y, hφ i k (homOfLE hik) y⟩
  · exact ⟨(F.map (homOfLE hjk)).hom y, hφ j k (homOfLE hjk) y⟩

/-- The ranges of the legs of a colimit cocone cover its point. -/
private lemma iSup_range_legHom_eq_top {c : Cocone F} (hc : IsColimit c) :
    (⨆ i : J, LinearMap.range (legHom c i)) = ⊤ := by
  set N := ⨆ i : J, LinearMap.range (legHom c i)
  have key : ModuleCat.ofHom N.mkQ = (0 : c.pt ⟶ ModuleCat.of R (↥c.pt ⧸ N)) := by
    refine hc.hom_ext fun i => ?_
    rw [comp_zero]
    ext y
    exact (Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero N).2 (Submodule.mem_iSup_of_mem i ⟨y, rfl⟩)
  exact eq_top_iff.2 fun x _ =>
    (Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero N).1 (by simpa using congr(($key).hom x))

variable [IsDirectedOrder J] [Nonempty J]

/-- Legs whose ranges cover the cocone point are jointly surjective, the family of those ranges
being directed. -/
private lemma exists_legHom_eq_of_iSup_range_eq_top {c : Cocone F}
    (hsurj : (⨆ i : J, LinearMap.range (legHom c i)) = ⊤) (x : c.pt) :
    ∃ (i : J) (y : F.obj i), legHom c i y = x := by
  obtain ⟨i, y, hy⟩ := (Submodule.mem_iSup_of_directed _
    (directed_range_of_forall_comp (legHom c) fun i j u y => legHom_map_apply c u y)).1
      (hsurj.ge Submodule.mem_top)
  exact ⟨i, y, hy⟩

/-- The legs of a colimit cocone over a nonempty directed system are jointly surjective. -/
private lemma exists_legHom_eq {c : Cocone F} (hc : IsColimit c) (x : c.pt) :
    ∃ (i : J) (y : F.obj i), legHom c i y = x :=
  exists_legHom_eq_of_iSup_range_eq_top (iSup_range_legHom_eq_top hc) x

end Cocone

/-! ### The direct limit maps injectively to the colimit -/

section DirectLimit

variable {R : Type u} [Ring R] {J : Type v} [Preorder J] [DecidableEq J]
  {F : J ⥤ ModuleCat.{w} R}

/-- The colimit of `F` computed elementwise, as an unbundled direct limit; it lives in
`Type (max v w)`, so it is not the point of a cocone on `F` in `ModuleCat.{w} R`. -/
private abbrev directLimit (F : J ⥤ ModuleCat.{w} R) : Type (max v w) :=
  Module.DirectLimit (fun i => (F.obj i : Type w)) fun _ _ h => (F.map (homOfLE h)).hom

/-- The canonical map from the member at `i` to the direct limit. -/
private noncomputable abbrev directLimitOf (F : J ⥤ ModuleCat.{w} R) (i : J) :
    (F.obj i : Type w) →ₗ[R] directLimit F :=
  Module.DirectLimit.of R J (fun i => (F.obj i : Type w)) (fun _ _ h => (F.map (homOfLE h)).hom) i

/-- The canonical maps into the direct limit absorb the transition maps. -/
private lemma directLimitOf_map_apply {i j : J} (u : i ⟶ j) (y : F.obj i) :
    directLimitOf F j ((F.map u).hom y) = directLimitOf F i y := by
  rw [map_eq_map F u (homOfLE (leOfHom u))]
  exact Module.DirectLimit.of_f

/-- The comparison map from the direct limit of `F` to the point of a cocone on `F`. -/
private noncomputable def directLimitDesc (c : Cocone F) : directLimit F →ₗ[R] (c.pt : Type w) :=
  Module.DirectLimit.lift R J _ _ (legHom c) fun _ _ h y => legHom_map_apply c (homOfLE h) y

/-- The comparison map sends the canonical image of `y` in the direct limit to the leg at `i`
applied to `y`. -/
private lemma directLimitDesc_directLimitOf (c : Cocone F) (i : J) (y : F.obj i) :
    directLimitDesc c (directLimitOf F i y) = legHom c i y := by
  simp [directLimitDesc]

/-- A linear map from the direct limit of `F` to an object of `ModuleCat.{w} R` factors through the
point of a colimit cocone: it is a cocone on `F` in `ModuleCat.{w} R`, so the universal property
applies to it, even though the direct limit itself is too large to be one. -/
private lemma exists_comp_directLimitOf {c : Cocone F} (hc : IsColimit c) {E : ModuleCat.{w} R}
    (Ψ : directLimit F →ₗ[R] (E : Type w)) :
    ∃ d : (c.pt : Type w) →ₗ[R] (E : Type w),
      ∀ (i : J) (y : F.obj i), d (legHom c i y) = Ψ (directLimitOf F i y) := by
  let t : Cocone F :=
    { pt := E
      ι :=
        { app := fun i => ModuleCat.ofHom (Ψ ∘ₗ directLimitOf F i)
          naturality := fun i j u => by
            ext y
            simpa using congrArg Ψ (directLimitOf_map_apply u y) } }
  exact ⟨(hc.desc t).hom, fun i y => congr($(hc.fac t i) y)⟩

/-- **The content of this file.** The comparison map from the elementwise direct limit of a
directed system of `R`-modules to the point of a colimit cocone is injective, the ring being
`w`-small: a nonzero element of its kernel is detected by a linear map into an injective object
of `ModuleCat.{w} R`, which factors through the cocone point and therefore kills it. -/
private lemma injective_directLimitDesc [Small.{w} R] [IsDirectedOrder J] {c : Cocone F}
    (hc : IsColimit c) : Function.Injective (directLimitDesc c) := by
  rw [injective_iff_map_eq_zero]
  intro x hx
  by_contra hne
  obtain ⟨E, Ψ, hΨ⟩ := exists_linearMap_apply_ne_zero R hne
  obtain ⟨d, hd⟩ := exists_comp_directLimitOf hc Ψ
  refine hΨ ?_
  have hfac : Ψ = d ∘ₗ directLimitDesc c :=
    Module.DirectLimit.hom_ext fun i => LinearMap.ext fun y => by
      simp only [LinearMap.comp_apply, directLimitDesc_directLimitOf, hd i y]
  rw [hfac, LinearMap.comp_apply, hx, map_zero]

omit [DecidableEq J] in
/-- An element of a member of a directed system of `R`-modules which is killed by its leg into the
colimit is killed by some transition map: `Ker(f i) = ⋃_{k ≥ i} Ker(f i k)`. -/
private lemma exists_map_eq_zero [Small.{w} R] [IsDirectedOrder J] {c : Cocone F}
    (hc : IsColimit c) (i : J) (z : F.obj i) (hz : legHom c i z = 0) :
    ∃ (k : J) (v : i ⟶ k), (F.map v).hom z = 0 := by
  classical
  have := directedSystem_map F
  have h0 : directLimitOf F i z = 0 :=
    injective_directLimitDesc hc (by rw [directLimitDesc_directLimitOf, hz, map_zero])
  obtain ⟨k, hik, hk⟩ := Module.DirectLimit.of.zero_exact h0
  exact ⟨k, homOfLE hik, hk⟩

end DirectLimit

/-! ### Recognising a colimit cocone -/

section Colimit

variable {R : Type u} [Ring R] {J : Type v} [Preorder J] [IsDirectedOrder J] [Nonempty J]
  {F : J ⥤ ModuleCat.{w} R}

/-- The descent map: a cocone `c` whose legs are jointly surjective and whose kernels are exhausted
by the transition maps maps to every cocone `t` on `F`, compatibly with the legs. The map is built
as the graph of itself, the supremum `Γ` of the ranges of the maps `(f i, t.ι.app i)`, whose first
projection is bijective by the two hypotheses; this avoids choosing a preimage. -/
private lemma exists_linearMap_comp_legHom {c t : Cocone F}
    (hsurj : ∀ x : (c.pt : Type w), ∃ (i : J) (y : F.obj i), legHom c i y = x)
    (hker : ∀ (i : J) (z : F.obj i), legHom c i z = 0 →
      ∃ (j : J) (v : i ⟶ j), (F.map v).hom z = 0) :
    ∃ d : (c.pt : Type w) →ₗ[R] (t.pt : Type w),
      ∀ (i : J) (z : F.obj i), d (legHom c i z) = legHom t i z := by
  set Γ : Submodule R (↥c.pt × ↥t.pt) :=
    ⨆ i : J, LinearMap.range ((legHom c i).prod (legHom t i))
  have hdir : Directed (· ≤ ·)
      fun i : J => LinearMap.range ((legHom c i).prod (legHom t i)) :=
    directed_range_of_forall_comp (fun i => (legHom c i).prod (legHom t i)) fun i j u y => by
      simp [legHom_map_apply c u y, legHom_map_apply t u y]
  have hmemΓ : ∀ (i : J) (z : F.obj i), (legHom c i z, legHom t i z) ∈ Γ := fun i z =>
    Submodule.mem_iSup_of_mem i ⟨z, rfl⟩
  have hmem : ∀ x : Γ, ∃ (i : J) (z : F.obj i),
      (legHom c i z, legHom t i z) = (x : ↥c.pt × ↥t.pt) := fun x =>
    (Submodule.mem_iSup_of_directed _ hdir).1 x.2
  have hbij : Function.Bijective ((LinearMap.fst R (↥c.pt) (↥t.pt)) ∘ₗ Γ.subtype) := by
    refine ⟨(injective_iff_map_eq_zero _).2 fun x hx => ?_, fun x => ?_⟩
    · obtain ⟨i, z, hz⟩ := hmem x
      have h1 : legHom c i z = 0 := by simpa using (congrArg Prod.fst hz).trans hx
      obtain ⟨j, v, hv⟩ := hker i z h1
      have h2 : legHom t i z = 0 := by rw [← legHom_map_apply t v z, hv, map_zero]
      exact Subtype.ext (by rw [← hz, h1, h2]; rfl)
    · obtain ⟨i, y, hy⟩ := hsurj x
      exact ⟨⟨_, hmemΓ i y⟩, hy⟩
  set e := LinearEquiv.ofBijective _ hbij
  refine ⟨(LinearMap.snd R (↥c.pt) (↥t.pt)) ∘ₗ Γ.subtype ∘ₗ e.symm.toLinearMap, fun i z => ?_⟩
  have h1 : e.symm (legHom c i z) = ⟨(legHom c i z, legHom t i z), hmemΓ i z⟩ :=
    e.injective (by rw [e.apply_symm_apply]; rfl)
  simp [h1]

/-- A cocone on a directed system of `R`-modules whose legs are jointly surjective and whose
kernels are exhausted by the transition maps is a colimit cocone. -/
private noncomputable def isColimit_of_iSup_range_of_ker {c : Cocone F}
    (hsurj : (⨆ i : J, LinearMap.range (legHom c i)) = ⊤)
    (hker : ∀ (i : J) (z : F.obj i), legHom c i z = 0 →
      ∃ (j : J) (v : i ⟶ j), (F.map v).hom z = 0) :
    IsColimit c := by
  have hjs := exists_legHom_eq_of_iSup_range_eq_top hsurj
  refine IsColimit.ofExistsUnique fun t => ?_
  obtain ⟨d, hd⟩ := exists_linearMap_comp_legHom hjs hker
  refine ⟨ModuleCat.ofHom d, fun i => ModuleCat.hom_ext (LinearMap.ext (hd i)), fun m hm => ?_⟩
  refine ModuleCat.hom_ext (LinearMap.ext fun x => ?_)
  obtain ⟨i, y, rfl⟩ := hjs x
  simpa [hd i y] using congr($(hm i) y)

end Colimit

/-! ### A countable directed subset closed under a map -/

section Tower

variable {J : Type v} [Preorder J]

/-- The `n`-th stage of the countable directed closure of a subset `B` of a directed preorder: at
each step one adjoins the values of `g` and, for each pair of elements, the upper bound `u`. -/
private def tower (g : J → J) (u : J → J → J) (B : Set J) : ℕ → Set J
  | 0 => B
  | n + 1 => tower g u B n ∪ g '' tower g u B n ∪
      Set.image2 u (tower g u B n) (tower g u B n)

omit [Preorder J] in
private lemma tower_mono (g : J → J) (u : J → J → J) (B : Set J) : Monotone (tower g u B) :=
  monotone_nat_of_le_succ fun _ _ hx => Or.inl (Or.inl hx)

omit [Preorder J] in
private lemma tower_countable (g : J → J) (u : J → J → J) {B : Set J} (hB : B.Countable) (n : ℕ) :
    (tower g u B n).Countable := by
  induction n with
  | zero => exact hB
  | succ n ih => exact ((ih.union (ih.image g)).union (ih.image2 ih u))

/-- A countable subset of a directed preorder is contained in a countable directed subset closed
under a prescribed map. -/
private lemma exists_countable_directedOn_superset [IsDirectedOrder J] (g : J → J) {B : Set J}
    (hB : B.Countable) :
    ∃ s : Set J, B ⊆ s ∧ s.Countable ∧ DirectedOn (· ≤ ·) s ∧ ∀ i ∈ s, g i ∈ s := by
  choose u hu₁ hu₂ using fun i j : J => exists_ge_ge i j
  refine ⟨⋃ n, tower g u B n, Set.subset_iUnion_of_subset 0 le_rfl,
    Set.countable_iUnion fun n => tower_countable g u hB n, ?_, ?_⟩
  · intro i hi j hj
    obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := Set.mem_iUnion.1 hi
    obtain ⟨m, hm⟩ := Set.mem_iUnion.1 hj
    exact ⟨u i j, Set.mem_iUnion.2 ⟨max n m + 1, Or.inr ⟨i, tower_mono g u B (le_max_left n m) hn,
      j, tower_mono g u B (le_max_right n m) hm, rfl⟩⟩, hu₁ i j, hu₂ i j⟩
  · intro i hi
    obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := Set.mem_iUnion.1 hi
    exact Set.mem_iUnion.2 ⟨n + 1, Or.inl (Or.inr ⟨i, hn, rfl⟩)⟩

end Tower

/-! ### Stacks 059W -/

/-- Stacks 059W: if the system factors eventually — for every `i` some transition map
`F.map u : F i ⟶ F j` factors through every `F.map v : F i ⟶ F k` — and the point of the colimit
cocone `c` is countably generated, then the restriction of `c` to some nonempty countable directed
subset of `J` is still a colimit cocone. This is the statement of
`ModuleCat.exists_countable_directedOn_isColimit_whisker`. -/
public theorem solution
    {R : Type u} [Ring R] [Small.{w} R] {J : Type v} [Preorder J] [IsDirectedOrder J] [Nonempty J]
    {F : J ⥤ ModuleCat.{w} R}
    (hfac : ∀ i : J, ∃ (j : J) (u : i ⟶ j), ∀ (k : J) (v : i ⟶ k),
      ∃ h : F.obj k ⟶ F.obj j, F.map u = F.map v ≫ h)
    {c : Cocone F} (hc : IsColimit c)
    (hcg : ∃ t : Set c.pt, t.Countable ∧ Submodule.span R t = ⊤) :
    ∃ s : Set J, s.Nonempty ∧ s.Countable ∧ DirectedOn (· ≤ ·) s ∧
      Nonempty (IsColimit (c.whisker (Set.mono_coe s).functor)) := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨T, hTc, hTspan⟩ := hcg
  have := hTc.to_subtype
  choose ig y hy using fun x : T => exists_legHom_eq hc (x : c.pt)
  choose jj uu huu using hfac
  obtain ⟨s, hBs, hsc, hsdir, hsclosed⟩ :=
    exists_countable_directedOn_superset jj
      ((Set.countable_range ig).insert (Classical.arbitrary J))
  have hi₀ : Classical.arbitrary J ∈ s := hBs (Set.mem_insert _ _)
  have hig : ∀ x : T, ig x ∈ s := fun x => hBs (Set.mem_insert_of_mem _ ⟨x, rfl⟩)
  have : Nonempty ↥s := ⟨⟨_, hi₀⟩⟩
  have : IsDirectedOrder ↥s := ⟨fun a b => by
    obtain ⟨k, hk, hak, hbk⟩ := hsdir a a.2 b b.2
    exact ⟨⟨k, hk⟩, hak, hbk⟩⟩
  refine ⟨s, ⟨_, hi₀⟩, hsc, hsdir, ⟨isColimit_of_iSup_range_of_ker ?_ ?_⟩⟩
  · refine eq_top_iff.2 (hTspan.ge.trans (Submodule.span_le.2 fun x hx => ?_))
    exact Submodule.mem_iSup_of_mem ⟨ig ⟨x, hx⟩, hig ⟨x, hx⟩⟩ ⟨y ⟨x, hx⟩, hy ⟨x, hx⟩⟩
  · intro i z hz
    obtain ⟨k, v, hv⟩ := exists_map_eq_zero hc (i : J) z hz
    obtain ⟨h, hh⟩ := huu (i : J) k v
    exact ⟨⟨jj (i : J), hsclosed (i : J) i.2⟩, homOfLE (leOfHom (uu (i : J))),
      map_eq_zero_of_map_comp_eq hh hv⟩

end Stacks.DirectLimit.CountableSubsystem

end

/-!
# Countable subsystems of a directed system of modules

Let `R` be a ring and let `(M i, f i j)` be a directed system of `R`-modules indexed by a directed
preorder `J`, that is a functor `F : J ⥤ ModuleCat R`, and let `M = colim_{i ∈ J} M i`. Say the
system *factors eventually* if for every `i` there is a `j ≥ i` such that the transition map
`f i j : M i → M j` factors through `f i k : M i → M k` for every `k ≥ i`. If the system factors
eventually and `M` is generated by countably many elements, then some countable directed subset
`J' ⊆ J` already computes `M`: the cocone on the restricted system `(M i)_{i ∈ J'}` obtained by
keeping the canonical maps `M i → M` is again a colimit cocone, so `M = colim_{i ∈ J'} M i`.

The point is that `J` may be arbitrarily large while `J'` is countable, so that `M` is the colimit
of a system indexed by a countable directed set — and hence, a countable directed set having a
cofinal chain, of a sequence `M 1 → M 2 → M 3 → ⋯`.

For a directed system of finitely presented modules, factoring eventually is condition (3) of
Stacks 059E, one of the five equivalent forms of the assertion that the colimit `M` is a
Mittag-Leffler module (Stacks 059F). So for such a system the hypothesis below reads "`M` is
Mittag-Leffler", and the statement is the source's.

## Main results

* `ModuleCat.exists_countable_directedOn_isColimit_whisker`: a directed system of `R`-modules that
  factors eventually and whose colimit `M` is countably generated has a countable directed
  subsystem with the same colimit `M`, with the same canonical maps.

## Implementation notes

Departures from the source's form, none of them weakening it.

The Mittag-Leffler hypothesis is written as condition (3) of Stacks 059E — for every `i`, some
transition map out of `M i` factors through all of them — rather than as "`M` is Mittag-Leffler".
The two agree for a directed system of finitely presented modules, which is the situation of the
source, by the equivalence (1) ⟺ (3) of that proposition. Condition (3) is what the source's own
proof of this lemma uses, and, unlike (1), it is a condition on the system rather than on `M`
alone, so no auxiliary presentation of `M` has to be chosen to state it.

Finite presentation of the members `M i` is not assumed: the source's proof never uses it, what it
does use is exactly condition (3) above, and so the lemma is stated at that generality. Under the
source's hypotheses the members are finitely presented and the hypothesis specialises to the
source's, as described above.

"Write `M = colim_{i ∈ I} M i`" is given as an arbitrary colimit cocone `c` on `F` together with
`IsColimit c`, rather than by choosing a construction of the colimit; `M` is then `c.pt` and the
canonical maps `M i → M` are the legs of `c`. This is what lets the conclusion pin the source's
canonical isomorphism rather than assert an abstract one: the cocone on the restricted system is
`c` whiskered along the inclusion of `J'`, that is `M` again, with the very same legs, and the
claim is that this cocone is a colimit cocone. As `IsColimit` is data, it is wrapped in `Nonempty`.

*Universes.* The ring `R` lives in its own universe `u` and is assumed `Small.{w}` over the
universe `w` of the members of the system, the convention of 059E, 059M, 059N, 05CQ and 0AS7; the
index `J` is left in its own universe `v`, nothing here needing it small. `Small.{w} R` is not a
weakening of the source, where the ring, the index set and the modules are sets in one universe:
it is automatic when `w = max u w`, in particular whenever `R` and the members share a universe,
so the single-universe form of the lemma is the instance `w := max u w`. It is not decoration
either. It is what makes the step the source's proof turns on — an element of `M i` killed by the
canonical map `f i : M i → M` is killed by some transition map `f i k`, that is
`Ker(f i) = ⋃_{k ≥ i} Ker(f i k)` — a theorem: the image of `M i` in the concrete colimit is then
`w`-small, so it embeds in an injective object of `ModuleCat.{w} R` and the kernel can be read off
a Baer extension. Without smallness somewhere that step is not known to hold, the factorisations
`M k → M j` of `f i j` having then to be chosen compatibly over a directed set of uncountable
cofinality, so the unrestricted statement is not a generality this lemma may be stated at.

The index `J` is a directed preorder rather than a directed partial order, and a directed subset is
a `Set J` satisfying `DirectedOn (· ≤ ·)`; antisymmetry is nowhere used. Countable generation of
`M` is written inline as the existence of a countable spanning set, since Mathlib has no predicate
for it; this is equivalent to the cardinal bound `(⊤ : Submodule R M).spanRank ≤ Cardinal.aleph0`
by `Submodule.spanRank_span_le_card` and `Submodule.exists_span_set_card_eq_spanRank`, and to the
`Module.CountablyGenerated` of `StacksFrontier/RingTheory/Flat/FaithfullyFlat/Descent.lean`, which
is the spelling the siblings 059X, 05A6 and 05A9 use. The ring is not assumed commutative.

Faithfulness. (1) No hypothesis is dead: directedness of `J` is what enlarges a countable subset to
a countable directed one; `Nonempty J` is what makes `J'` nonempty, as a directed set is; factoring
eventually is what makes the comparison map injective, and it is used for indices `i ∈ J'` against
all `k ∈ J`, not only those in `J'`; countable generation of `M` is what makes it surjective;
`Small.{w} R` is used as described above. No necessity is claimed for the factoring hypothesis: by
the remark following Stacks 059W, the module `R/(x_1, x_2, …)` over `R = k[x_1, x_2, …]` is the
colimit of a countable system of finitely presented modules without being Mittag-Leffler. (2) The
scalars are the source's: one ring `R` throughout, `F` takes values in `R`-modules, the transition
maps and the factorisations are `R`-linear, and the spanning set of `M` spans over `R`. (3) The
canonical maps are pinned, not merely asserted to exist: the colimit cocone `c` is part of the
data, and the conclusion is about `c` itself, restricted to `J'`. (4) The instantiation is not
degenerate: take `k` an uncountable field, `M = ⨁_{n ∈ ℕ} k`, `J` the uncountable directed set of
finite-dimensional subspaces of `M` and `F` the system of their inclusions, whose colimit is `M`;
the system factors eventually, with `j = i`, because a subspace of a vector space is a direct
summand of every subspace containing it, and `M` is countably generated, while the conclusion has
content, the countable `J'` — the standard flag, say — being far from cofinal in `J`.

## References

* [StacksProject 059W](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/059W)
-/



